  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- A warm front will bring widespread light snowfall through this\n  evening.\n\n- Monday through Wednesday trends drier with warming in the\n  mountains.\n\n- Next system potentially impacting travel over passes on\n  Thanksgiving morning.\n\nLight precipitation has been fallen across the region this\nmorning, watching cameras over by Spokane WA produced accumulating\nsnow on grassy surfaces and wet roads. As the precipitation shifts\nmore into western MT this afternoon the result will likely be\nsimilar as temperatures are right around freezing and intensities\nare expected to remain light. \n\nA ridge of high pressure will build over the region from Monday \nthrough Wednesday, promoting the development of strengthening \nvalley inversions. The primary hazards during this timeframe will \nbe freezing fog and low stratus. These inversions may be strong \nenough to trap fog and low clouds in the valleys throughout the \nday, which will significantly limit daytime heating and high \ntemperatures.\n\nThanksgiving morning the next system arrives, snow levels around\n4000 feet initially will slowly rise throughout the morning. This\nsystem will be associated with weak IVT, better known as a \natmospheric river. Expect a breezy Thanksgiving afternoon, with \nlight precipitation in valleys due to strong valley down sloping. \nThe mountains will receive steady precipitation through Saturday \nwith a few inches of snow at Lolo, Lookout, and Lost Trail Passes \nfor Thanksgiving morning. While our forecast is calling for only a\nfew inches currently, these systems are notorious for over \npreforming. Keep apprised of future forecasts for your travel \nneeds.\n\nBy Friday, snow levels will be between 5 and 6000 feet. A few more\ninches of snow will be possible above this elevation.",
